-- Complementing and negating a type narrower than the register that holds it.
--
-- `U8` and `U16` fill 8 and 16 bits of a 32-bit register, so a value of one is held zero-extended
-- and every operation that can set a bit above the declared width has to put it back - which is
-- `wrapsAtDeclaredWidth`'s list and `truncateToWidth` doing it. `not` is the bitwise operation on
-- that list: `and`, `or` and `xor` of two in-range operands stay in range and a complement sets
-- every bit there is, so `not(0 :: U8)` is 255 and not a register of ones.
--
-- A *signed* narrow value is held sign-extended and its complement already is, so `not(1 :: I8)` is
-- -2 with or without the wrap. It is here as the row that says the wrap costs the signed side
-- nothing rather than as the row that fails.
--
-- Every operand goes through a `let &`, which is what keeps the constant folder from answering the
-- question at the type and hiding what the lowering does with it.
fn main() -> Int:
    let &byte = 0 :: U8
    let &short = 0 :: U16
    let &one = 1 :: U8
    let &signed = 1 :: I8

    -- 255 + 65535 + 255 - 2
    return (widen(not(byte)) :: Int) + (widen(not(short)) :: Int)
         + (widen(-one) :: Int) + (widen(not(signed)) :: Int)
